INFO:__main__:Prompt Metadata:
INFO:__main__:- name: AIAH Get Action Prompt
INFO:__main__:- description: This prompt is for the AIAH to use its knowledge (memory, consience and skills) and the environment status to take an action. This action will impact with environment
INFO:__main__:- requires: AIAH_memory, AIAH_skills, AIAH_conscience, ES_enviroment_status
INFO:__main__:Prompting GPT
INFO:httpx:HTTP Request: POST https://api.openai.com/v1/chat/completions "HTTP/1.1 200 OK"
INFO:__main__:
{
  "action": "Given the dire conditions with a severe blizzard, extremely low temperatures, and high danger from aggressive wildlife, the primary focus should be on survival by prioritizing warmth, hydration, and safety. With a body temperature critically low at 29.3 and energy severely depleted, my immediate actions are as follows: \n\n1. **Shelter and Warmth:** I will remain within the reinforced debris shelter, minimizing exposure to the harsh weather. Efforts to improve insulation using available materials should continue to conserve body heat and prevent hypothermia.\n\n2. **Hydration:** Utilize the technique of melting snow using body heat to secure water, being mindful of conserving energy to prevent further health deterioration.\n\n3. **Energy Conservation:** Avoid unnecessary movements or activities that could expend energy. Focus on resting and maintaining warmth within the shelter.\n\n4. **Wildlife Vigilance:** Continuously monitor for signs of scavengers near the shelter. Ensure the shelter is secured to prevent any wildlife intrusion.\n\n5. **Reassess Strategy:** Continuously reassess environmental conditions and adjust strategies accordingly, keeping an eye on weather patterns and any potential for improving conditions. \n\nThe current situation demands a strategic focus on maintaining core body temperature and hydration while minimizing exposure to the elements and potential wildlife threats. Adapting to the immediate environment and making incremental improvements to survival tactics will be crucial for enduring these extreme conditions."
}

